# video-watcher - optional environment settings
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Copy to .env and edit ONLY if the defaults do not work on your machine.
# Most people never need this file. Nothing here is required.
#
# The tool does not read .env automatically - it reads real environment
# variables. Set them in your shell, or load this file with your own tooling:
#
#   PowerShell   $env:VIDEO_WATCHER_FFMPEG = "C:\ffmpeg\bin\ffmpeg.exe"
#   bash / zsh   export VIDEO_WATCHER_FFMPEG="/usr/local/bin/ffmpeg"
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THERE ARE NO CREDENTIALS HERE, AND THERE IS NOWHERE TO PUT ANY.
#
# The pipeline talks to no API, holds no account, reads no browser profile and
# passes no cookies to yt-dlp. It downloads public video with public tools and
# processes it locally. If a fork of this ever asks you for an API key, a
# YouTube login or a --cookies-from-browser flag, that is not this tool.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Full path to the ffmpeg binary. Only needed when ffmpeg is installed but not
# on your PATH. Default: the "ffmpeg" found on PATH.
# VIDEO_WATCHER_FFMPEG=C:\ffmpeg\bin\ffmpeg.exe

# Full path to a bold sans TTF, used to burn timestamps into contact sheets.
# Default: auto-detected per OS. Set this if --check warns about the font.
# VIDEO_WATCHER_FONT=C:\Windows\Fonts\arialbd.ttf

# Where analyses are written. A relative path resolves against the package
# root (the folder holding README.md). Default: video-research
# Precedence: --root  >  VIDEO_WATCHER_ROOT  >  config.json "output_root"
# VIDEO_WATCHER_ROOT=video-research
